\f1\fs22 Alexander left his crew to the bump-in and rig of the Lysander Shakti Memorial Auditorium and decided to take lunch. He called for one of his mother\'92s limousines to take him up to the golf club eatery\'97not the most exclusive dining room in the sleepy little village, but the place where he\'92d be seen by more valuable eyeball assets than at any establishment requiring a) a certified family tree linking you back to royalty or a mate of Robert Menzies; or b) a stake in a space exploration brotherhood.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 As he sat in the back of the limo, and the gates opened for him, and he passed at a regally slow pace, Alexander waved at the UDF troops (some of whom waved back), the Elvi (none of whom waved back), and young Airborne (who\'92d  never notice another limo cruising around their elite enclave). The limo passed between fairways and shortly turned off the road and made for the clubhouse.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The limo pulled up at the members entrance and the commissionaire
\f2\b  
\f1\b0 opened his door.
\f2\b  
\f1\b0 Alexander tipped the kid in club livery with a smile, and perfunctorily waved at the staff who greeted him on the way to the dining room.\
\'93Good afternoon, sir,\'94 said the kid behind the host stand. \'93Your usual table?\'94\
By 
\f3\i usual table
\f1\i0 , the kid had diplomatically offered Alexander his mother\'92s table.\
Alexander morphed a grimace into a shallow smile. \'93You know, I think I\'92d like to take the view in from another vantage point.\'94\
\'93We\'92re rather full, sir,\'94 the kid said warily.\
\'93I\'92ll wait.\'94\
The smile washed from the kid\'92s face. \'93Of course sir, one minute.\'94\
Within forty seconds an indignant party of four were marching out of the dining area, and a squad of waiters were resetting a table by the window.\
\'93This way please, sir.\'94\
Once seated at the table, Alexander scanned the proffered menu.\
The 
\f3\i ma\'eetre d\'92h\'f4tel
\f1\i0  appeared at Alexander\'92s shoulder. \
Alexander couldn\'92t remember his name. \'93I\'92ll have the pangolin,\'94 he said and flicked the menu up at the waiter like he was raising a disqualification flag at an athletics meet.\
\'93Very good, sir.\'94\
\'93And have the sommelier fetch me a glass of something.\'94\
The 
\f3\i ma\'eetre d\'92
\f1\i0  nodded and was about to retire when Alexander held up his hand to make him wait.\
\'93What\'92s all the fuss about in here?\'94\
The 
\f3\i ma\'eetre d\'92
\f1\i0  rolled his eyes. \'93It\'92s the UDF, sir. The major has commandeered our best function room upstairs, and we\'92re trying to accommodate all the displaced bookings. Frankly, he\'92s thrown our complete Christmas booking schedule into disarray.\'94\
\'93I see.\'94 Alexander thought for a moment. \'93I might go pay him a little visit after lunch.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Major Midler had moved his command post to the golf clubhouse, commandeering the function room with the best view across the course. He\'92d since been served several court orders to vacate by irate families and interests whose partying plans he had ruined\'97a wedding, a biker gang breakfast, a BDSM club\'92s Christmas munch, a masonic lodge\'92s annual handshake practice, and the 
\f3\i bat mitzvah
\f1\i0  of a delightful girl who threatened to financially ruin him and his descendants for seven generations.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 War was hell. Of the threats of the community groups whose functions he\'92d displaced, his tactical assessment  ranked the masons second only to the Sweet Little Jewish Girl.\
A voice was raised, out in the hall\'97Major Midler could hear it all from the far corner of the function room that had been partitioned into a makeshift office. He stood to investigate and saw an expensive suit and haircut burst through the function-room doors followed by a pair of armed uniforms who were ineffectively attempting to lay the gentlest of hands on him.\
Major Midler recognised that face, if not from the pile of dossiers in his filing cabinet, then from the intrusive ads he saw for an evening TV game show that purported to try and execute villains while you watch. He held up his hand to calm his staff.\
The intruder\'97Alexander Shakti\'97smoothed his suit and ran his fingers through his hair.\
\'93I\'92m sorry,\'94 Alexander said. \'93Have I come at a bad time?\'94\
Midler looked Alexander over. \'93Are you armed?\'94\
Alexander gave Midler a smile worthy of a toothpaste commercial and said, \'93Only if you count intelligence as a deadly weapon.\'94\
Midler weighed a pause before saying, \'93In other circumstances, perhaps. Come in.\'94\
Alexander shrugged the guards away and took his stroll past desks and screens, cabinets and uniforms. Midler returned to his makeshift office and offered Alexander a seat.\
Once planted, Alexander didn\'92t offer or wait for pleasantries. \'93I believe you may have surrounded my ancestral seat,\'94 he said in the same gameshow-pleasant voice that had helped win him an unfairly insufficient number of Gold Budgie awards.\
\'93These are not matters I can discuss with you, Mr Shakti,\'94 Midler said, and waited.\
Alexander decided to go straight to it. \'93Show me the script.\'94\
\'93The what?\'94 However Midler thought the conversation would develop, he mentally filed it all under 
\fs20 PLANS KILLED IN ACTION
\fs22 .\
\'93The script.\'94 Alexander saw the dimness in the major\'92s eyes. \'93Or at least a treatment\'85 I mean\'85 Look, just bring in your Producer\'97the real one, not any 
\f3\i Executive
\f1\i0  Producer\'85 if you get my drift.\'94\
Midler swallowed and tried to reset. \'93Mr Shakti, please explain the purpose of your visit.\'94\
\'93You need me.\'94\
\'93What?\'94\
\'93Look, I can see this is a budget production\'97\'94\
\'93I am a major in the Ulyssian Defence Force. I assure you I have a perfectly adequate budget.\'94\
Alexander pulled a face that expressed extreme doubt. \'93Well, where\'92re all your cameras? Your sound and lighting? I mean, I see soldiers and guns and trucks and bivouacs but nothing to record the action on.\'94\
\'93We won\'92t be recording anything,\'94 Midler retorted, offended. He couldn\'92t recall the last time his composure had capitulated after so little resistance.\
\'93Well, what kind of a waste is that?\'94 Alexander challenged. \'93I mean, you break out all your toys, and you haven\'92t thought for one second how you can make yourself famous.\'94\
\'93I assure you the UDF is not here to make anyone famous.\'94\
Alexander scoffed and stood. \'93Look. I don\'92t know what you have planned, but I assure you, everything will have changed after the show.\'94\
Midler looked at him sideways, Alexander took it to mean more explaining was required. \'93My 
\f3\i final 
\f1\i0 Candid Criminal ep ever. A Christmas Special, streamed live from six this evening. Tune in, then you\'92ll see real intelligence. Intelligence you  can actually use. You might even get to go home and see your kids for Christmas.\'94
